WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.880
Success metrics, failure metrics, and non-goals.

2
00:00:03.160 --> 00:00:06.403
A metric turns “it works” into something you can compare.

3
00:00:06.683 --> 00:00:21.573
Google’s problem-framing course makes a distinction worth adopting: product success metrics describe what changes for the user (time saved, errors avoided, tasks completed) and are separate from model metrics such as accuracy or recall.

4
00:00:21.853 --> 00:00:27.229
You need both, and you should check that improving the model metric would actually move the product metric.

5
00:00:27.509 --> 00:00:33.525
If better retrieval recall wouldn’t change whether the designer trusts the output, recall isn’t your headline.

6
00:00:33.805 --> 00:00:42.893
The same course recommends defining a failure metric alongside success, with targets for both, so the space between them is small and explicit.

7
00:00:43.173 --> 00:00:58.811
For a compliance-matrix builder: success is a complete matrix in under an hour with no missed mandatory requirements on held-out RFPs; failure is any missed mandatory requirement, or a matrix that takes longer to check than to write by hand.

8
00:00:59.091 --> 00:01:04.936
Writing the failure condition down in week 1 protects you from redefining success in week 6.

9
00:01:05.216 --> 00:01:19.573
Martin Zinkevich’s Rules of Machine Learning, written from Google’s production experience, puts metrics second only to “don’t be afraid to launch without machine learning,” and recommends a simple, observable, attributable first objective.

10
00:01:19.853 --> 00:01:34.125
For a capstone, that means one headline metric you can compute on every eval case, plus a small number of guard metrics (cost per task, p95 latency, attack success rate, wrong-abstention rate) that must not get worse.

11
00:01:34.405 --> 00:01:35.771
Then write the non-goals.

12
00:01:36.051 --> 00:01:52.947
Malte Ubl’s description of design docs at Google treats non-goals (things that could reasonably be goals but that you are deliberately excluding) as a core section, and Google’s Technical Writing One course gives the same advice for any document: state the scope and the non-scope.

13
00:01:53.227 --> 00:01:55.296
“Does not support scanned PDFs.

14
00:01:55.576 --> 00:01:57.880
” “Does not send email; drafts only.

15
00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.269
” “English only.

16
00:01:59.549 --> 00:02:05.843
” Each non-goal is a feature you have already said no to, which makes the next request to add it easy to decline.
